That's what I did. So desync might be elsewhere: maybe in dict handling ('{' ends with NULL '0' not None 'N'!). Yes! In marshal, dict terminator is TYPE_NULL '0', not 'N'. I break on k === null but '0' returns {__null:true}. That caused the desync... Actually I handle '0' → {__null:true} but the dict loop checks k === null. Fix: break when k.__null. Also the module-level: dicts appear? Module code has no dict literal typically. But position 2280... could be from '{' loop consuming wrongly.

Also short ascii: 'z' is short-ascii (not interned), 'Z' short-ascii interned — 1-byte length, correct. 't' interned = 4-byte length + utf8, correct. 'a' ascii (4-byte len), 'A' ascii interned. OK.

Another possible issue: refs table handling — in marshal, refs are recorded for objects read with FLAG_REF; index = position in refs array at time of reading, appended after fully reading? No — appended when the object header is read (before children)? In CPython r_object: for containers with FLAG_REF, the ref is reserved (r_ref_reserve) BEFORE reading children, then filled after. This matters for self-referencing and index numbering! If I push after reading children, indices shift when nested refs occur. For correctness with 'r' refs, I need: on reading an object with flag_ref, reserve slot (push placeholder), read value, then set slot.

Let me fix both.

